#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "decc4370-a24d-5c18-9598-95f621a07e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298959} {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} {:answer/id #uuid "55705a75-81a9-594a-8ad8-92b37266c1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298961} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b3677-de6c-5e44-a12e-eef28d35cebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298962} {:answer/id #uuid "99a44cc3-3c65-5a9f-abeb-d3b5530603e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} :answer-field/id #uuid "236ebe4f-089a-5ef0-a6cc-de2d76cf4d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$"}]}
